<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Compose owns theming (Material 3 — M10). This XML theme exists only so the
      Activity has a window before Compose takes over; it deliberately does not
      depend on the Material Components view library.
    -->
    <!--
      The parent must be Theme.DeviceDefault.DayNight: there is no
      `.DayNight.NoActionBar` in the framework. AOSP publishes exactly two
      DayNight styles (Theme.DeviceDefault.DayNight and
      ThemeOverlay.DeviceDefault.Accent.DayNight); every DeviceDefault
      NoActionBar variant is non-DayNight. An unresolvable parent is an aapt2
      link error, not a warning. So the action bar is removed by attribute.
    -->
    <!--
      ⭐ M10 — windowBackground is the redesign's canvas, and it is NOT cosmetic.
      This is the colour the framework paints between the launcher tap and the
      first Compose frame. Left as DayNight's default it is *white* on a device
      in light mode, so opening a note flashed a full-screen white rectangle
      before a near-black editor — the one moment of the app the theme cannot
      reach from Kotlin. Fixed here because there is nowhere else to fix it.

      It is a literal rather than a colour resource so that this file stays the
      only place a launch-time colour is defined; `Ink.Canvas` in InkTokens.kt
      carries the same value and the KDoc there says so.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Inkstone" parent="android:Theme.DeviceDefault.DayNight">
        <item name="android:windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#09090B</item>
        <item name="android:colorBackground">#09090B</item>
        <!--
          ⚠️ Android 12+ draws its own splash and does NOT read windowBackground
          for it. Measured on the emulator: with only the two attributes above,
          launching still showed the icon on a **white** field for about a second
          before the near-black library appeared. `windowSplashScreenBackground`
          is the attribute that governs it, and it needs the API 31 qualifier
          because it does not exist below that — this project's minSdk is 30, so
          an unqualified entry is an aapt2 link error against the base SDK.
          See res/values-v31/themes.xml.
        -->
        <!--
          The design is dark-first and has no light twin, so the system bars'
          icons are light unconditionally. `enableEdgeToEdge()`'s default is
          `SystemBarStyle.auto`, which reads the *device's* day/night setting —
          on a tablet in light mode that gives dark status-bar icons over a
          near-black canvas, i.e. an invisible clock. The activities pass an
          explicit dark style for the same reason; these two attributes cover
          the window before Compose exists.
        -->
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
